<<^ code="&#x0002;">>

<<~ ahu #/the-claim>>

! Corpus Conformance ~ a run, not a claim

A spec that says //the grammar is X// asks a reader to believe it. A spec that says //this corpus obeys
X, and here is the command// hands the reader an instrument. The difference decides whether an outside
reviewer can check the work without learning the house first.

''The run answers ONE question:'' does this corpus obey the grammar THIS seed declares?

''And it answers it about a NAMED state.'' A conformance verdict carries the seed's own block check
(`ni:///sha-256;…`) as its first field. Without that the verdict names no grammar — //conforms// to
what? Two runs that disagree may both read true against different seeds, and a reader with only the
booleans cannot tell that from a regression.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/what-stands>>

!! The ground this design starts from

Nothing here proposes a new instrument. The surface exists and sits unbound.

| ''standing'' | ''count'' | ''shape'' |
|shell witnesses |10 |`bcc` · `doctype` · `frame-shape` · `ahu-sections` · `authoring` · `meme-coordinates` · `round-trip` · `submission-parity` · `type-parity` · `typecheck` |
|corpus-walking predicates |9 |`carrier-shape` · `frames-per-carrier` · `grammar-in-a-wiki` · `ahu-sections-address` · `carrier-edges` · `child-name-blocks` · `bracket-law-one-spelling` · `vm-grammar-boundary` · `meme-corpus-roundtrip` |
|a runner that binds them |''0'' |— |
|a verdict tied to a seed-state |''0'' |— |
|a record any of them append to |''0'' |— |

''Three gaps, one shape:'' the house measures its corpus thoroughly and remembers nothing about the
measuring. Each witness answers alone, to a console, once.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-five-classes>>

!! The five law classes

A conformance run groups its laws by ''what a failure means'', never by which tool happens to hold them.
A reader who sees `frame` fail knows a carrier tore; a reader who sees `vocabulary` fail knows the
corpus lags the seed. Those want different hands.

!!! A ~ Frame laws — the carrier holds together

# A carrier opens by naming the grammar that reads it, at the one address that does.
# Every frame mark stands on the control head; every opened carrier closes.
# A carrier holds exactly one frame.
# A block check covers the bytes above it, or reports `unchecked` — never `pass`.

!!! B ~ Address laws — every name resolves

# Every child slot roots at its carrier (`#/name`, nested carrying its whole path).
# Every open carries a close.
# Every open resolves to a tiddler the render can address.
# Every form a carrier names an address in reads.
# Every meme coordinate resolves to a carrier that stands.

!!! C ~ Round-trip laws — the reader and the writer agree

# `render(parse(disk))` returns the body it was given.
# A carrier normalizes to itself: `normalize --check` reads canonical.
# One closer, idempotent, content-whole.

!!! D ~ Projection laws — what leaves the house

# Every pair on the submission shelf re-projects byte-identical from its source.

!!! E ~ Vocabulary laws — the corpus speaks THIS seed's grammar

''The missing class, and the one this design exists for.'' A and D pass over a corpus firing a sigil
the seed retired six months ago: the bytes hold, the frame stands, the projection matches, and the
grammar has drifted underneath all of it.

# No carrier fires a form the seed retired, EXCEPT a carrier that declares itself a record.
# Every sigil head the corpus fires is declared — by the seed, or by an `api/` carrier that stands.
# Every panel key the corpus writes appears in the seed's `set` signature, or reports as novel.

''The exempt set is DECLARED, never inferred.'' A record — a transcript, an experiment log, a campaign
scroll — quotes a grammar rather than speaking it, and a run that silently skipped such carriers would
report a clean corpus while hiding its own blind spot. The run NAMES what it exempted and why, in the
record it writes.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-drift-reading>>

!! The drift reading ~ why the record matters more than the verdict

''Conformance reads as a boolean at an instant and as a SHAPE across time.'' The seed moves — four times
in one session, on the day this was designed — and each move is a boundary, not a fault. Between two
seed-states a run reports:

* ''which laws changed'' — the grammar's own diff, stated as laws rather than bytes;
* ''how many carriers moved'' to meet them, and over how long;
* ''the LAG'' — carriers still speaking the prior state, per form.

''The lag curve IS the region signal.'' A span where lag returns to zero and stays names a semi-stable
region; a spike names a boundary. That shape — tooth in the scroll, a rhythm in what the house does to
itself — reads as exactly the structure the sensorium exists to detect, and it arrives free once the run
appends rather than prints.

So the run MUST emit a record, not a verdict alone:

```toml
seed-check   = "ni:///sha-256;…"        # WHICH grammar
corpus-size  = 699                      # carriers walked
exempt       = ["records: 2"]           # named, never silent
laws         = { frame = "pass", address = "pass", roundtrip = "pass",
                 projection = "pass", vocabulary = "lag:58" }
lag-by-form  = { "confidence-sigil" = 53, "standalone-ward" = 5 }
```

A file of those, one per run, IS the time series. Nothing else needs building for the sensorium to read
the house's own grammar-history the way it reads a chant.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/failure-discipline>>

!! Failure discipline ~ four rules the instruments earned

Each rule below was paid for by a measured failure, not reasoned from principle.

# ''A law that cannot run reports `unchecked`, never `pass`.'' Absence and conformance name different
facts, and a run that collapsed them would read cleanest exactly where it saw least. The block check
already holds this line; every law inherits it.
# ''A law names the CARRIERS that failed, never a count alone.'' A count sends a reader back to the
grep that produced it; a list sends them to the bytes.
# ''A run over a corpus that moved mid-run reads VOID, never partial.'' Chaining a witness behind a
writer measures the writer: three phantom failures appeared that way in one session, each vanishing on
a quiet read. The run takes the corpus's digest at open and at close, and voids itself if they differ.
# ''A probe narrower than the reader it guards is a FAILING gate, not a passing one.'' Measured four
times in one night — a gate matching `#name` where the reader takes `#/name`; nine readers spelling a
literal space the parser never required; a word-boundary matching inside `#meta-ha`; a file-list grep
tighter than the matcher it fed, which hid every law carrier and showed only the records. So the run
carries a cross-check: for each law, run the LAW's pattern and the READER's pattern over the same
corpus, and report where the counts diverge. A silent agreement proves nothing; a divergence names a
gate that has been reporting green about a question it never asked.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/what-it-must-not-say>>

!! What a conformance run MUST NOT claim

# ''Never that the grammar is correct.'' The run reads a corpus against a seed. A seed that states a
bad law yields a clean run.
# ''Never that the spec is settled.'' The seed-check names ONE state; a series of them names movement,
and movement reads as structure rather than as failure.
# ''Never that no retired form exists.'' It reports what it walked and what it exempted, and a reader
who disagrees with the exempt set can re-run without it.
# ''Never a verdict an outside reviewer cannot reproduce.'' The command, the seed-check and the corpus
digest travel together, or the claim travels alone.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-door>>

!! The door

```
lares corpus conform [--seed <carrier>] [--exempt <glob>…] [--emit <record>] [--cross-check]
```

''Staged, each stage standing alone:''

# ''Bind what stands.'' One runner over the ten witnesses and nine predicates; one verdict; the record
written. No new law, and the first record dated from a corpus already measured.
# ''Add class E.'' Vocabulary conformance — the retired-form sweep this house has been running by hand
becomes a law with an exempt set stated in the open.
# ''Add the cross-check.'' Each law's probe measured against its reader's, divergence reported.
# ''Read the series.'' The sensorium reads the emitted records as a rhythm; the lag curve names the
regions, and the boundaries name the days the grammar turned.

''Stage 1 pays for itself immediately'': the submissions track stops arguing by projection and starts
citing a run. Stages 2–4 buy what no witness gives today — a house that remembers how its own grammar
moved.

<<~/ahu>>

